<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_Ch5Sec4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Integration Formulas and the Net Change Theorem</title>
  <introduction>
  <title> Learning Objectives</title>
  <ul>
<li>Apply the basic integration formulas.</li>
<li>Explain the significance of the net change theorem.</li>
<li>Use the net change theorem to solve applied problems.</li>
<li>Apply the integrals of odd and even functions.</li>
</ul>
<p id="fs-id1170572470452">In this section, we use some basic antiderivative formulas studied previously to solve some key applied problems involving definite integrals.  The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus establishes a link between differentiation and integration:  We can use integrals to construct an antiderivative of any continuous function on a closed interval, and we can use antiderivatives to evaluate definite integrals.  Using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2 as motivation, we introduce the following notation</p>
<definition xml:id="def-indefinite-integral">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>f(x)</m> be continuous over an interval. The <term>indefinite integral</term> of <m>f(x)</m> represents the most general antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m> and is denoted</p>
<md>\displaystyle\int f(x)\, dx</md>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>Although definite and indefinite integrals are closely related via the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, they are different mathematical objects. A definite integral is a <em style="italic">number</em>, representing the net signed area under a curve over an interval. An indefinite integral represents an <em style="italic">infinite family of functions</em>, all of which differ by a constant, and each of which is an antiderivative of the same function.  The similar choice of notation helps emphasize and highlight the statements of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, yet the integral symbol itself can indicate two very different objects.</p>
<p>  As a reminder, antiderivatives give us an efficient way to evaluate definite integrals, but the definition of a definite integral does not require an antiderivative.  There may be situations where we are unable to identify a useful form of an antiderivative and where we may be unable to apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2, directly.  All the same, a definite integral may exist despite our inability to compute it effectively.  Some examples include </p>
<md>\int_{1}^{2} \sqrt{1+x^4}\, dx, \quad \displaystyle\int_0^{\pi} \sin(x^3)\, dx, \quad \text{and} \quad \displaystyle\int_0^1 e^{x^2}\, dx</md>
<p>When an elementary antiderivative is out of reach, we can return to the definition of the definite integral as a limit of Riemann sums, or use numerical methods to approximate the net signed area. Later in calculus, we will broaden our notion of functions to include <em>power series</em>, giving us a new way to represent the exact values of these three integrals.</p>
</introduction>

<subsection id="fs-id1170572280538">
<title>Basic Integration Formulas</title>
<p>Recall the basic antiderivative formulas given in the <xref ref="fs-id1165043092431">table in Antiderivatives</xref> and the <xref ref="fs-id1170571711362" text="title"/> in <xref ref="sec_Ch5Sec2"/>. Let's look at a few examples of how to apply these rules.</p>

<example xml:id="fs-id1170571609338">
  <title>Integrating a Function Using the Power Rule</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Use the power rule to evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_1^4 \sqrt{t}(1+t)\, dt</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>The first step is to rewrite the integrand and simplify it so we can apply the power rule.  We can distribute to write the integrand as a sum of powers of the variable <m>t</m>:</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_1^4 \sqrt{t}(1+t)\, dt \amp= \int_1^4 t^{1/2}(1+t)\, dt</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \int_1^4  t^{1/2} + t^{3/2} \, dt.</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>Now apply the power rule:</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_1^4  t^{1/2} + t^{3/2} \, dt \amp=  \frac{2}{3}t^{3/2} + \frac{2}{5}t^{5/2} \biggr\vert_1^4</mrow>
      <mrow>&amp;= \left(\frac{2}{3}(4)^{3/2} + \frac{2}{5}(4)^{5/2}\right) - \left(\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2} + \frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}\right)</mrow>
      <mrow>&amp;= \frac{256}{15}.</mrow>
    </md>
  </solution>
</example>


<exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572558558">
  <statement>
    <p>Use the power rule to evaluate <m>\displaystyle\int_1^3 x^2 - 3x\, dx</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>Follow the process from <xref ref="fs-id1170571609338"/> to solve the problem.</p>
  </hint>
  <solution>
    <p><m>-\frac{10}{3}</m></p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<example xml:id="ln-antiderivative">
  <title>Integrating a Function Using a Known Antiderivative</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Use a known antiderivative to evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_2^8 \dfrac{1}{x} \, dx</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>Because <m>\ln \vert x \vert</m> is an antiderivative of <m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m>, we can apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2, and use properties of logarithms to simplify:</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_2^8 \frac{1}{x}\, dx \amp= \ln\vert x \vert\biggr\vert_2^8</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \ln \vert 8 \vert - \ln \vert 2\vert </mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \ln \left( \frac{8}{2} \right)</mrow>
       <mrow>\amp= \ln 4</mrow>
    </md>
  </solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="secantsquared-antiderivative">
  <title>Integrating a Trigonometric Function Using a Known Antiderivative</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Use a known antiderivative to evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_0^{\pi/4} \sec^2 \theta \, d\theta</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>Because <m>\tan\theta</m> is an antiderivative of <m>\sec^2\theta</m>, we can apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2:</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_0^{\pi/4} \sec^2 \theta \, d\theta \amp= \tan\theta\biggr\vert_0^{\pi/4}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \tan(\pi/4)-\tan 0 </mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= 1-0</mrow>
       <mrow>\amp= 1</mrow>
    </md>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="ex-integrate-cos">
  <statement>
    <p>Use a known antiderivative to evaluate <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{\pi/2} \cos x\, dx</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>Follow the process from <xref ref="secantsquared-antiderivative"/> to solve the problem.  Don't forget that both limits of integration contribute to the value of the integral!</p>
  </hint>
  <solution>
    <p><m>1</m></p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<example xml:id="ex-linearity">
  <title>Applying Linearity Properties of the Definite Integral</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_0^3 3e^x-x\, dx</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>We can use properties of the definite integral to write</p>
    <md>\int_0^3 3e^x-x\, dx = 3\int_0^3 e^x \, dx- \int_0^3 x \, dx </md>
    <p>
      Because <m>e^x</m> is an antiderivative of <m>e^x</m>, we have
    </p>
    <md><mrow>3\int_0^3 e^x \, dx \amp= 3\left(e^x\biggr\vert_0^3\right)</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp= 3(e^3-e^0) </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp= 3e^3-3 </mrow>
    </md>
    <p>The power rule gives:</p>
    <md><mrow>\int_0^3 x \, dx \amp= \frac{1}{2}x^2\biggr\vert_0^3</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp= \frac{1}{2}(3)^2-\frac{1}{2}(0)^2 </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp= \frac{9}{2}</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>Putting everything together, we have</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_0^3 3e^x-x\, dx \amp= 3\int_0^3 e^x \, dx- \int_0^3 \, dx </mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= 3e^3-3 - \frac{9}{2}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= 3e^3-\frac{15}{2}</mrow>
    </md>
  </solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-algebraic-rewrite-definite">
  <title>Rewriting an Integrand Before Applying Basic Rules</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_1^2 \frac{x^3 - 4x + 1}{x^2}\, dx</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>The integrand does not yet match a known antiderivative. we first divide each term in the numerator by <m>x^2</m>, and then use linearity properties of the definite integral to decompose the problem into simpler pieces:</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_1^2 \frac{x^3 - 4x + 1}{x^2}\, dx \amp= \int_1^2 x - \frac{4}{x} + x^{-2}\, dx</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \int_1^2 x\, dx - 4\int_1^2 \frac{1}{x}\, dx + \int_1^2 x^{-2}\, dx.</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>Applying the power rule to the first and third integrals and the natural logarithm rule to the middle integral gives</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_1^2 x\, dx \amp= \frac{1}{2}x^2\biggr\vert_1^2 = \frac{1}{2}(2)^2 - \frac{1}{2}(1)^2 = \frac{3}{2},</mrow>
      <mrow>4\int_1^2 \frac{1}{x}\, dx \amp= 4\left(\ln\vert x\vert\biggr\vert_1^2\right) = 4(\ln 2 - \ln 1) = 4\ln 2,</mrow>
      <mrow>\int_1^2 x^{-2}\, dx \amp= -x^{-1}\biggr\vert_1^2 = -\frac{1}{2} - (-1) = \frac{1}{2}.</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>Combining these three results, we obtain</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_1^2 \frac{x^3 - 4x + 1}{x^2}\, dx \amp= \frac{3}{2} - 4\ln 2 + \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= 2 - 4\ln 2.</mrow>
    </md>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="tan-squared">
  <statement>
    <p>Rewrite the integrand to use known antiderivatives to evaluate <m>\displaystyle\int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \tan^2 x\, dx</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
  <p>We do not yet have a basic antiderivative formula for <m>\tan^2 x</m>, but in <xref ref="secantsquared-antiderivative"/> we integrated <m>\sec^2 \theta</m>. How are <m>\tan^2 x</m> and <m>\sec^2 x</m> related?</p>
</hint>
  <solution>
    <p><m>2 - \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m></p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="fs-id1170572627233">
  <title>The Net Change Theorem</title>
  <p>When a function <m>F</m> describes a changing quantity, the net change in <m>F</m> as <m>x</m> changes from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m> (or over the interval <m>[a,b]</m>) is</p>
     <md>F(b)-F(a)</md>
   <p> Here it is helpful to think of the number <m>F(a)</m> as a starting value and the number <m>F(b)</m> as an ending value.  The net change in <m>F</m> is the amount that must be added to the starting value <m>F(a)</m> to obtain the ending value <m>F(b)</m>:</p>
    <md>F(b)=F(a)+\underbrace{F(b)-F(a)}_{\text{net change}}</md>
   <p> The <term>Net Change Theorem</term> connects the net change in a function over an interval to the integral of its <em>rate of change</em> (a derivative).  </p>
  <theorem xml:id="fs-id1170572295126">
    <title>Net Change Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>F</m> is a continuously differentiable function on an interval <m>[a, b]</m>.  The integral <m>\displaystyle\int_a^b F'(x)\, dx</m> is the net change in <m>F(a)</m> over the interval <m>[a,b]</m>:</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\int_a^b F'(x)\, dx = F(b) - F(a)</mrow>
        <mrow>\text{or}</mrow>
        <mrow>F(b) = F(a) + \int_a^b F'(x)\, dx.</mrow>
      </md>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>If the Net Change Thorem looks familiar, it is because this is a direct application of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2, by viewing <m>F(x)</m> as an antiderivative of <m>F'(x)</m>.</p>

  <p>The significance of the net change theorem lies in the results. Net change can be applied to area, distance, and volume, to name only a few applications. Net change accounts for negative quantities automatically without having to write more than one integral. To illustrate, let's apply the net change theorem to a <term>velocity</term> function in which the result is <term>displacement</term>.</p>

  <p>We looked at a simple example of this in <xref ref="sec_Ch5Sec2"/>. Suppose a car is moving due north (the positive direction) at <m>40</m> mph between <m>2</m> p.m. and <m>4</m> p.m., then the car moves south at <m>30</m> mph between <m>4</m> p.m. and <m>5</m> p.m. We can graph this motion as shown in <xref ref="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_001"/>.</p>

  <figure xml:id="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_001">
    <caption>The graph shows velocity versus time for the given motion of a car.</caption>
    <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_002.jpg" width="50%">
      <description>A graph with the horizontal axis marked as t and the vertical axis marked v(t). The horizontal line segments y = 40 and y = -30 are drawn over [2, 4] and [4, 5], respectively. The areas between the line segments and the horizontal axis are shaded.</description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>Just as we did before, we can use definite integrals to calculate the net displacement as well as the total distance traveled. The net displacement is given by</p>
  <md>
    <mrow>\int_2^5 v(t)\, dt \amp= \int_2^4 40\, dt + \int_4^5 -30\, dt</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp= 80 - 30</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp= 50.</mrow>
  </md>

  <p>Thus, at <m>5</m> p.m. the car is <m>50</m> mi north of its starting position. The total distance traveled is given by</p>
  <md>
    <mrow>\int_2^5 \lvert v(t) \rvert\, dt \amp= \int_2^4 40\, dt + \int_4^5 30\, dt</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp= 80 + 30</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp= 110.</mrow>
  </md>

  <p>Therefore, between <m>2</m> p.m. and <m>5</m> p.m., the car traveled a total of <m>110</m> mi.</p>

  <p>To summarize, net displacement may include both positive and negative values. In other words, the velocity function accounts for both forward distance and backward distance. To find net displacement, integrate the velocity function over the interval. Total distance traveled, on the other hand, is always positive. To find the total distance traveled by an object, regardless of direction, we need to integrate the absolute value of the velocity function (the <em>speed</em> of the object).</p>

<example xml:id="fs-id1170572229863">
  <title>Finding Net Displacement</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Given a velocity function <m>v(t) = 3t - 5</m> (in meters per second) for a particle in motion from time <m>t = 0</m> to time <m>t = 3</m>, find the net displacement of the particle.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>Applying the net change theorem, we have</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_0^3 (3t - 5)\, dt \amp= \left.\left(\frac{3t^2}{2} - 5t\right)\right|_0^3</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \left[\frac{3(3)^2}{2} - 5(3)\right] - 0</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \frac{27}{2} - 15</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \frac{27}{2} - \frac{30}{2}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= -\frac{3}{2}.</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>The net displacement is <m>-\frac{3}{2}</m> m (<xref ref="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_002"/>).</p>
    <figure xml:id="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_002">
      <caption>The graph shows velocity versus time for a particle moving with a linear velocity function.</caption>
      <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_003.jpg" width="50%">
        <description>A graph of the line v(t) = 3t - 5, which goes through points (0, -5) and (5/3, 0). The area over the line and under the horizontal axis in the interval [0, 5/3] is shaded. The area under the line and above the horizontal axis in the interval [5/3, 3] is shaded.</description>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="fs-id1170572247866">
  <title>Finding the Total Distance Traveled</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Use <xref ref="fs-id1170572229863"/> to find the total distance traveled by a particle according to the velocity function <m>v(t) = 3t - 5</m> m/sec over a time interval <m>[0, 3]</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>The total distance traveled includes both the positive and the negative values. Therefore, we must integrate the absolute value of the velocity function to find the total distance traveled.</p>
    <p>To continue with the example, use two integrals to find the total distance. First, find the <m>t</m>-intercept of the function, since that is where the division of the interval occurs. Set the equation equal to zero and solve for <m>t</m>. Thus,</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>3t - 5 \amp= 0</mrow>
      <mrow>3t \amp= 5</mrow>
      <mrow>t \amp= \frac{5}{3}.</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>The two subintervals are <m>\left[0, \frac{5}{3}\right]</m> and <m>\left[\frac{5}{3}, 3\right]</m>. To find the total distance traveled, integrate the absolute value of the function. Since the function is negative over the interval <m>\left[0, \frac{5}{3}\right]</m>, we have <m>\lvert v(t) \rvert = -v(t)</m> over that interval. Over <m>\left[\frac{5}{3}, 3\right]</m>, the function is positive, so <m>\lvert v(t) \rvert = v(t)</m>. Thus, we have</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int_0^3 \lvert v(t) \rvert\, dt \amp= \int_0^{5/3} -v(t)\, dt + \int_{5/3}^3 v(t)\, dt</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \int_0^{5/3} (5 - 3t)\, dt + \int_{5/3}^3 (3t - 5)\, dt</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \left.\left(5t - \frac{3t^2}{2}\right)\right|_0^{5/3} + \left.\left(\frac{3t^2}{2} - 5t\right)\right|_{5/3}^3</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \left[5\left(\frac{5}{3}\right) - \frac{3(5/3)^2}{2}\right] - 0 + \left[\frac{27}{2} - 15\right] - \left[\frac{3(5/3)^2}{2} - \frac{25}{3}\right]</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \frac{25}{3} - \frac{25}{6} + \frac{27}{2} - 15 - \frac{25}{6} + \frac{25}{3}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \frac{41}{6}.</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>So, the total distance traveled is <m>\dfrac{41}{6}</m> m.</p>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572622550">
  <statement>
    <p>Find the net displacement and total distance traveled in meters given the velocity function <m>f(t) = \frac{1}{2}e^t - 2</m> over the interval <m>[0, 2]</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>Follow the procedures from <xref ref="fs-id1170572229863"/> and <xref ref="fs-id1170572247866"/>. Note that <m>f(t) \leq 0</m> for <m>t \leq \ln 4</m>, and <m>f(t) \geq 0</m> for <m>t \geq \ln 4</m>.</p>
  </hint>
  <solution>
    <p>Net displacement: <m>\dfrac{e^2 - 9}{2} \approx -0.8055</m> m; total distance traveled: <m>4\ln 4 - 7.5 + \frac{e^2}{2} \approx 1.740</m> m.</p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<p>The net change theorem also applies to the flow and consumption of fluids, as shown in <xref ref="fs-id1170571542385"/>.</p>

  <example xml:id="fs-id1170571542385">
    <title>How Many Gallons of Gasoline Are Consumed?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>If the motor on a motorboat is started at <m>t = 0</m> and the boat consumes gasoline at the rate of <m>5 - t^3</m> gal/hr, how much gasoline is used in the first <m>2</m> hours?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Express the problem as a definite integral, integrate, and evaluate using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. The limits of integration are the endpoints of the interval <m>[0, 2]</m>. We have</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\int_0^2 (5 - t^3)\, dt \amp= \left.\left(5t - \frac{t^4}{4}\right)\right|_0^2</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \left[5(2) - \frac{(2)^4}{4}\right] - 0</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= 10 - \frac{16}{4}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= 6.</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Thus, the motorboat uses <m>6</m> gal of gas in <m>2</m> hours.</p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="fs-id1170572420100">
    <title>Chapter Opener: Iceboats</title>
    <statement>
      <figure xml:id="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_005">
        <caption>(credit: modification of work by Carter Brown, Flickr)</caption>
        <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_01_018.jpg" width="50%">
          <description>An image of an iceboat in action.</description>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>As we saw at the beginning of the chapter, top <term>iceboat</term> racers can attain speeds of up to five times the wind speed. Andrew is an intermediate iceboater, though, so he attains speeds equal to only twice the wind speed. Suppose Andrew takes his iceboat out one morning when a light <m>5</m>-mph breeze has been blowing all morning. As Andrew gets his iceboat set up, though, the wind begins to pick up. During his first half hour of iceboating, the wind speed increases according to the function <m>v(t) = 20t + 5</m>. For the second half hour of Andrew's outing, the wind remains steady at <m>15</m> mph. In other words, the wind speed is given by</p>
      <md>
        v(t) = \begin{cases}
          20t + 5 \amp \text{for } 0 \leq t \leq \frac{1}{2} \\
          15 \amp \text{for } \frac{1}{2} \leq t \leq 1.
        \end{cases}
      </md>
      <p>Recalling that Andrew's iceboat travels at twice the wind speed, and assuming he moves in a straight line away from his starting point, how far is Andrew from his starting point after <m>1</m> hour?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>To figure out how far Andrew has traveled, we need to integrate his velocity, which is twice the wind speed. Then</p>
      <md>\text{Distance} = \int_0^1 2v(t)\, dt.</md>
      <p>Substituting the expressions we were given for <m>v(t)</m>, we get</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\int_0^1 2v(t)\, dt \amp= \int_0^{1/2} 2v(t)\, dt + \int_{1/2}^1 2v(t)\, dt</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \int_0^{1/2} 2(20t + 5)\, dt + \int_{1/2}^1 2(15)\, dt</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \int_0^{1/2} (40t + 10)\, dt + \int_{1/2}^1 30\, dt</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \left.\left(20t^2 + 10t\right)\right|_0^{1/2} + \left.30t\right|_{1/2}^1</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{20}{4} + 5\right) - 0 + (30 - 15)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= 25.</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Andrew is <m>25</m> mi from his starting point after <m>1</m> hour.</p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572379540">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that, instead of remaining steady during the second half hour of Andrew's outing, the wind starts to die down according to the function <m>v(t) = -10t + 15</m>. In other words, the wind speed is given by</p>
      <md>
        v(t) = \begin{cases}
          20t + 5 \amp \text{for } 0 \leq t \leq \frac{1}{2} \\
          -10t + 15 \amp \text{for } \frac{1}{2} \leq t \leq 1.
        \end{cases}
      </md>
      <p>Under these conditions, how far from his starting point is Andrew after <m>1</m> hour?</p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Don't forget that Andrew's iceboat moves twice as fast as the wind.</p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p><m>17.5</m> mi</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="fs-id1170571711320">
  <title>Integrating Even and Odd Functions</title>
  <p>We saw in Chapter 1 that an <term>even function</term> is a function in which <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m> for all <m>x</m> in the domain—that is, the graph of the curve is unchanged when <m>x</m> is replaced with <m>-x</m>. The graphs of even functions are symmetric about the <m>y</m>-axis. An <term>odd function</term> is one in which <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m> for all <m>x</m> in the domain, and the graph of the function is symmetric about the origin.</p>

  <p>Integrals of even functions, when the limits of integration are from <m>-a</m> to <m>a</m>, involve two equal areas, because they are symmetric about the <m>y</m>-axis. Integrals of odd functions, when the limits of integration are similarly <m>[-a, a]</m>, evaluate to zero because the areas above and below the <m>x</m>-axis are equal.</p>

  <note xml:id="fs-id1170572217489">
    <title>Rule: Integrals of Even and Odd Functions</title>
    <p>For continuous even functions such that <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m>,</p>
    <md>\int_{-a}^a f(x)\, dx = 2\int_0^a f(x)\, dx.</md>
    <p>For continuous odd functions such that <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m>,</p>
    <md>\int_{-a}^a f(x)\, dx = 0.</md>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="fs-id1170572624845">
    <title>Integrating an Even Function</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Integrate the even function <m>\displaystyle \int_{-2}^2 (3x^8 - 2)\, dx</m> and verify that the integration formula for even functions holds.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The symmetry appears in the graphs in <xref ref="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_003"/>. Graph (a) shows the region below the curve and above the <m>x</m>-axis. We have to zoom in to this graph by a huge amount to see the region. Graph (b) shows the region above the curve and below the <m>x</m>-axis. The signed area of this region is negative. Both views illustrate the symmetry about the <m>y</m>-axis of an even function. We have</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\int_{-2}^2 (3x^8 - 2)\, dx \amp= \left.\left(\frac{x^9}{3} - 2x\right)\right|_{-2}^2</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \left[\frac{(2)^9}{3} - 2(2)\right] - \left[\frac{(-2)^9}{3} - 2(-2)\right]</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{512}{3} - 4\right) - \left(-\frac{512}{3} + 4\right)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \frac{1000}{3}.</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>To verify the integration formula for even functions, we can calculate the integral from <m>0</m> to <m>2</m> and double it, then check to make sure we get the same answer.</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\int_0^2 (3x^8 - 2)\, dx \amp= \left.\left(\frac{x^9}{3} - 2x\right)\right|_0^2</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \frac{512}{3} - 4</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \frac{500}{3}.</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Since <m>2 \cdot \frac{500}{3} = \frac{1000}{3}</m>, we have verified the formula for even functions in this particular example.</p>
      <figure xml:id="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_003">
        <caption>Graph (a) shows the positive area between the curve and the <m>x</m>-axis, whereas graph (b) shows the negative area between the curve and the <m>x</m>-axis. Both views show the symmetry about the <m>y</m>-axis.</caption>
        <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_005.jpg" width="50%">
          <description>Two graphs of the same function f(x) = 3x^8 - 2, side by side. It is symmetric about the y-axis, has x-intercepts at (-1, 0) and (1, 0), and has a y-intercept at (0, -2). The function decreases rapidly as x increases until about -0.5, where it levels off at -2. Then, at about 0.5, it increases rapidly as a mirror image. The first graph is zoomed-out and shows the positive area between the curve and the x-axis over [-2, -1] and [1, 2]. The second is zoomed-in and shows the negative area between the curve and the x-axis over [-1, 1].</description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="fs-id1170571599733">
    <title>Integrating an Odd Function</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Evaluate the definite integral of the odd function <m>-5\sin x</m> over the interval <m>[-\pi, \pi]</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The graph is shown in <xref ref="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_004"/>. We can see the symmetry about the origin by the positive area above the <m>x</m>-axis over <m>[-\pi, 0]</m>, and the negative area below the <m>x</m>-axis over <m>[0, \pi]</m>. We have</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} -5\sin x\, dx \amp= -5(-\cos x)\biggr\vert_{-\pi}^{\pi}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= 5\cos x\biggr\vert_{-\pi}^{\pi}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \bigl[5\cos \pi\bigr] - \bigl[5\cos(-\pi)\bigr]</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= -5 - (-5)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= 0.</mrow>
      </md>
      <figure xml:id="CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_004">
        <caption>The graph shows areas between a curve and the <m>x</m>-axis for an odd function.</caption>
        <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_04_006.jpg" width="50%">
          <description>A graph of the given function f(x) = -5 sin(x). The area under the function but above the x-axis is shaded over [-pi, 0], and the area above the function and under the x-axis is shaded over [0, pi].</description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572337805">
    <statement>
      <p>Integrate the function <m>\displaystyle \int_{-2}^2 x^4\, dx</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Integrate an even function.</p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p><m>\frac{64}{5}</m></p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="fs-id1170572337865">
  <title>Key Concepts</title>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>The net change theorem states that when a quantity changes, the final value equals the initial value plus the integral of the rate of change. Net change can be a positive number, a negative number, or zero.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>The area under an even function over a symmetric interval can be calculated by doubling the area over the positive <m>x</m>-axis. For an odd function, the integral over a symmetric interval equals zero, because half the area is negative.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="fs-id1170572274778">
  <title>Key Equations</title>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p><term>Net Change Theorem</term></p>
      <md>\int_a^b F'(x)\, dx = F(b) - F(a) \quad \text{or} \quad F(b) = F(a) + \int_a^b F'(x)\, dx</md>
    </li>
  </ul>
</subsection>

<exercises xml:id="fs-id1170571733925">
  <exercisegroup>
    <introduction>
      <p>Use basic integration formulas to compute the following definite integrals.</p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571733932">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\displaystyle \int_1^4 \left(\sqrt{x} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\right)\, dx</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <md>
          <mrow>\int_1^4 \left(\sqrt{x} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\right)\, dx \amp= \int_1^4 x^{1/2}\, dx - \int_1^4 x^{-1/2}\, dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \left.\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}\right|_1^4 - \left.2x^{1/2}\right|_1^4</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{16}{3} - \frac{2}{3}\right) - (4 - 2)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{8}{3}</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572233918">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\displaystyle \int_0^2 \left(e^{2x} - \frac{1}{2}e^{x/2}\right)\, dx</m></p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572622517">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\displaystyle \int_1^e \frac{dx}{2x}</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <md>
          <mrow>\int_1^e \frac{dx}{2x} \amp= \frac{1}{2}\ln\lvert x \rvert\biggr\vert_1^e</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2}(\ln e - \ln 1)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572334286">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\displaystyle \int_1^2 \frac{x - 1}{x^2}\, dx</m></p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572344266">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\displaystyle \int_0^{\pi} (\sin x - \cos x)\, dx</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <md>
          <mrow>\int_0^{\pi} (\sin x - \cos x)\, dx \amp= \int_0^{\pi} \sin x\, dx - \int_0^{\pi} \cos x\, dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= -\cos x\biggr\vert_0^{\pi} - \sin x\biggr\vert_0^{\pi}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \bigl(-(-1) + 1\bigr) - (0 - 0)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 2</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572444357">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\displaystyle \int_0^{\pi/2} (x - \sin x)\, dx</m></p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
</exercisegroup>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572293468">
    <statement>
      <p>Write an integral that expresses the increase in the perimeter <m>P(s)</m> of a square when its side length <m>s</m> increases from <m>2</m> units to <m>4</m> units and evaluate the integral.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>P(s) = 4s</m>, so <m>\frac{dP}{ds} = 4</m> and <m>\displaystyle \int_2^4 4\, ds = 8</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571613575">
    <statement>
      <p>Write an integral that quantifies the change in the area <m>A(s) = s^2</m> of a square when the side length doubles from <m>S</m> units to <m>2S</m> units and evaluate the integral.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572554393">
    <statement>
      <p>A regular <m>N</m>-gon (an <m>N</m>-sided polygon with sides that have equal length <m>s</m>, such as a pentagon or hexagon) has perimeter <m>Ns</m>. Write an integral that expresses the increase in perimeter of a regular <m>N</m>-gon when the length of each side increases from <m>1</m> unit to <m>2</m> units and evaluate the integral.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <md>
        <mrow>\int_1^2 N\, ds \amp= N</mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572628420">
    <statement>
      <p>The area of a regular pentagon with side length <m>a &gt; 0</m> is <m>pa^2</m> with <m>p = \frac{1}{4}\sqrt{5 + \sqrt{5 + 2\sqrt{5}}}</m>. The Pentagon in Washington, DC, has inner sides of length <m>360</m> ft and outer sides of length <m>920</m> ft. Write an integral to express the area of the roof of the Pentagon according to these dimensions and evaluate this area.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572543692">
    <statement>
      <p>A dodecahedron is a Platonic solid with a surface that consists of <m>12</m> pentagons, each of equal area. By how much does the surface area of a dodecahedron increase as the side length of each pentagon doubles from <m>1</m> unit to <m>2</m> units?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>With <m>p</m> as in the previous exercise, each of the <m>12</m> pentagons increases in area from <m>p</m> to <m>4p</m> square units, so the net increase in the surface area of the dodecahedron is <m>36p</m> square units.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572543727">
    <statement>
      <p>An icosahedron is a Platonic solid with a surface that consists of <m>20</m> equilateral triangles. By how much does the surface area of an icosahedron increase as the side length of each triangle doubles from <m>a</m> units to <m>2a</m> units?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572184318">
    <statement>
      <p>Write an integral that quantifies the change in the area of the surface of a cube when its side length doubles from <m>s</m> units to <m>2s</m> units and evaluate the integral.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <md>
        <mrow>18s^2 \amp= 6\int_s^{2s} 2x\, dx</mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571653387">
    <statement>
      <p>Write an integral that quantifies the increase in the volume of a cube when the side length doubles from <m>s</m> units to <m>2s</m> units and evaluate the integral.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571653450">
    <statement>
      <p>Write an integral that quantifies the increase in the surface area of a sphere as its radius doubles from <m>R</m> units to <m>2R</m> units and evaluate the integral.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <md>
        <mrow>12\pi R^2 \amp= 8\pi\int_R^{2R} r\, dr</mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571597447">
    <statement>
      <p>Write an integral that quantifies the increase in the volume of a sphere as its radius doubles from <m>R</m> units to <m>2R</m> units and evaluate the integral.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572480525">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that a particle moves along a straight line with velocity <m>v(t) = 4 - 2t</m>, where <m>0 \leq t \leq 2</m> (in meters per second). Find the displacement at time <m>t</m> and the total distance traveled up to <m>t = 2</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>\displaystyle d(t) = \int_0^t v(s)\, ds = 4t - t^2</m>. The total distance is <m>d(2) = 4</m> m.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572369345">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that a particle moves along a straight line with velocity defined by <m>v(t) = t^2 - 3t - 18</m>, where <m>0 \leq t \leq 6</m> (in meters per second). Find the displacement at time <m>t</m> and the total distance traveled up to <m>t = 6</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572373389">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that a particle moves along a straight line with velocity defined by <m>v(t) = \lvert 2t - 6 \rvert</m>, where <m>0 \leq t \leq 6</m> (in meters per second). Find the displacement at time <m>t</m> and the total distance traveled up to <m>t = 6</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>\displaystyle d(t) = \int_0^t v(s)\, ds</m>. For <m>t \lt 3</m>, <m>\displaystyle d(t) = \int_0^t (6 - 2s)\, ds = 6t - t^2</m>. For <m>t \gt 3</m>, <m>\displaystyle d(t) = d(3) + \int_3^t (2s - 6)\, ds = 9 + \left.(s^2 - 6s)\right|_3^t = t^2 - 6t + 18</m>.</p>
      <p>The total distance is <m>d(6) = 18</m> m.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571649968">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that a particle moves along a straight line with acceleration defined by <m>a(t) = t - 3</m>, where <m>0 \leq t \leq 6</m> (in meters per second squared). Find the velocity and displacement at time <m>t</m> and the total distance traveled up to <m>t = 6</m> if <m>v(0) = 3</m> and <m>d(0) = 0</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572235269">
    <statement>
      <p>A ball is thrown upward from a height of <m>1.5</m> m at an initial speed of <m>40</m> m/sec. Acceleration resulting from gravity is <m>-9.8</m> m/sec<m>^2</m>. Neglecting air resistance, solve for the velocity <m>v(t)</m> and the height <m>h(t)</m> of the ball <m>t</m> seconds after it is thrown and before it returns to the ground.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>v(t) = 40 - 9.8t</m> m/s; <m>h(t) = 1.5 + 40t - 4.9t^2</m> m</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571712591">
    <statement>
      <p>A ball is thrown upward from a height of <m>3</m> m at an initial speed of <m>60</m> m/sec. Acceleration resulting from gravity is <m>-9.8</m> m/sec<m>^2</m>. Neglecting air resistance, solve for the velocity <m>v(t)</m> and the height <m>h(t)</m> of the ball <m>t</m> seconds after it is thrown and before it returns to the ground.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572443658">
    <statement>
      <p>The area <m>A(t)</m> of a circular shape is growing at a constant rate. If the area increases from <m>4\pi</m> units to <m>9\pi</m> units between times <m>t = 2</m> and <m>t = 3</m>, find the net change in the radius during that time.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The net increase is <m>1</m> unit.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572419271">
    <statement>
      <p>A spherical balloon is being inflated at a constant rate. If the volume of the balloon changes from <m>36\pi</m> in.<m>^3</m> to <m>288\pi</m> in.<m>^3</m> between time <m>t = 30</m> and <m>t = 60</m> seconds, find the net change in the radius of the balloon during that time.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571699444">
    <statement>
      <p>Water flows into a conical tank with cross-sectional area <m>\pi x^2</m> at height <m>x</m> and volume <m>\frac{\pi x^3}{3}</m> up to height <m>x</m>. If water flows into the tank at a rate of <m>1</m> m<m>^3</m>/min, find the height of water in the tank after <m>5</m> min. Find the change in height between <m>5</m> min and <m>10</m> min.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>At <m>t = 5</m>, the height of water is <m>x = \left(\frac{15}{\pi}\right)^{1/3}</m> m. The net change in height from <m>t = 5</m> to <m>t = 10</m> is <m>\left(\frac{30}{\pi}\right)^{1/3} - \left(\frac{15}{\pi}\right)^{1/3}</m> m.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571679806">
    <introduction>
      <p>A horizontal cylindrical tank has cross-sectional area <m>A(x) = 4(6x - x^2)</m> m<m>^2</m> at height <m>x</m> meters above the bottom when <m>x \leq 3</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>The volume <m>V</m> between heights <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> is <m>\displaystyle \int_a^b A(x)\, dx</m>. Find the volume at heights between <m>2</m> m and <m>3</m> m.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that oil is being pumped into the tank at a rate of <m>50</m> L/min. Using the chain rule, <m>\frac{dx}{dt} = \frac{dx}{dV}\frac{dV}{dt}</m>, at how many meters per minute is the height of oil in the tank changing, expressed in terms of <m>x</m>, when the height is at <m>x</m> meters?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>How long does it take to fill the tank to <m>3</m> m starting from a fill level of <m>2</m> m?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

<exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571638194">
    <statement>
      <p>The following table lists the electrical power in gigawatts—the rate at which energy is consumed—used in a certain city for different hours of the day, in a typical <m>24</m>-hour period, with hour <m>1</m> corresponding to midnight to <m>1</m> a.m.</p>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <col/>
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Hour</cell>
          <cell>Power</cell>
          <cell>Hour</cell>
          <cell>Power</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          <cell><m>28</m></cell>
          <cell><m>13</m></cell>
          <cell><m>48</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell>
          <cell><m>25</m></cell>
          <cell><m>14</m></cell>
          <cell><m>49</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>3</m></cell>
          <cell><m>24</m></cell>
          <cell><m>15</m></cell>
          <cell><m>49</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>4</m></cell>
          <cell><m>23</m></cell>
          <cell><m>16</m></cell>
          <cell><m>50</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>24</m></cell>
          <cell><m>17</m></cell>
          <cell><m>50</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>6</m></cell>
          <cell><m>27</m></cell>
          <cell><m>18</m></cell>
          <cell><m>50</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>7</m></cell>
          <cell><m>29</m></cell>
          <cell><m>19</m></cell>
          <cell><m>46</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>8</m></cell>
          <cell><m>32</m></cell>
          <cell><m>20</m></cell>
          <cell><m>43</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>9</m></cell>
          <cell><m>34</m></cell>
          <cell><m>21</m></cell>
          <cell><m>42</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>10</m></cell>
          <cell><m>39</m></cell>
          <cell><m>22</m></cell>
          <cell><m>40</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>11</m></cell>
          <cell><m>42</m></cell>
          <cell><m>23</m></cell>
          <cell><m>37</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>12</m></cell>
          <cell><m>46</m></cell>
          <cell><m>24</m></cell>
          <cell><m>34</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <p>Find the total amount of energy in gigawatt-hours (GW-h) consumed by the city in a typical <m>24</m>-hour period.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The total daily energy consumption is estimated as the sum of the hourly power rates, or <m>911</m> GW-h.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571543244">
    <introduction>
      <p>The average residential electrical power use (in hundreds of watts) per hour is given in the following table.</p>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <col/>
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Hour</cell>
          <cell>Power</cell>
          <cell>Hour</cell>
          <cell>Power</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          <cell><m>8</m></cell>
          <cell><m>13</m></cell>
          <cell><m>12</m></cell>
